Z||000000||GTDude||12-10-2001||03:31 PM||||Thinking of putting a nitrous system on my 86 GT 2.8.  Does anyone have any experience with this?  Is it a good idea?  Any drawbacks?  Any brand better than another.  Thanks<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||MODIFIE||12-10-2001||03:42 PM||||nitrous will decrease the life of the engine and depending on how much nitrous you us you may have to have forge pistons, rods, and crank. Note I have never run nitrous just read about it, me myself I wouldn't run nitrous unless it was a strip only car. just my appenion||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||12-10-2001||04:03 PM||||...don't believe everything you  read! <P>i've been spraying V6 fieros for 4 years and other cars for 8 not a single nitrous related failure. <A HREF="http://www.nitrouswarehouse.com" TARGET=_blank>www.nitrouswarehouse.com</A>  kit 20920 $449!!! i've installed 15-20 kits and everyone is still working just as it did the day i installed it. on it's original engine. the stock V6 will take 75(wet) all day and not flinch! that'll put you in the mid 13s. with 0-60 in the 5s. <BR>  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fst2m6sig_1.gif">  <P> <A HREF="http://www.1fst2m6.com" TARGET=_blank>www.1fst2m6.com</A>  - underconstruction - but check it out anywho...<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 12-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||GTDude||12-10-2001||04:27 PM||||Wow, that sounds fantastic!  Can you tell me where you inject the nitrous?  I would think in the bellows going to the throttle body, but have no real idea.  How long did it take you to install your first one?  Are any other parts needed.  Thanks.....you've made my day!<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000004||Shaun41178||12-10-2001||05:58 PM||||You are an auto tech and you don't know where nitrous gets injected at?  My god man.<P>Yes it can be injected in the "bellows" right before the TB.  You can also have it setup to where it injects nitrous into each individual cylinder (direct port)<P>Do a search on nitrous and you will find many of your questions answered as far as installing a kit.<P>------------------<BR>Sold Nitrous 85 GT.<BR>Looking for another.<BR>FOR SALE: Aluminum v6 block.<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000005||GTDude||12-11-2001||07:18 AM||||Shaun.......yes silly boy...i know the possible places in which nitrous can be injected.  What I was asking was, with the recommended kit, where it should be injected. Thanks for your comments tho.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||GodSend||12-11-2001||09:46 AM||||I was wondering what the differences are in "difficulty of install" for a dry versus a wet system (I totally understand the dry system install, but I am lost on the wet system).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||GTDude||12-11-2001||11:19 AM||||well I'm totally lost then...I don't know the diff between wet and dry systems...could you explain please cause I'm gonna buy the sucker, its a killer price too.  Any additional advice would be appreciated. Thanks.<P>GTDude||||reg||3
Z||000008||1FST2M6||12-11-2001||02:28 PM||||there is NOT time difference installation-wise between wet and dry. NONE! <P><BR>Dry is nitrous only with a partial provision for fuel. WET injects atomized fuel along with the nitrous. (THE best, safest and stongest way to do it) ||||reg||1
Z||000009||GodSend||12-11-2001||02:47 PM||||I see.  This may be a stupid question.  In a dry system, the nos comes from the bottle and is injected into the intake "as is" - In a wet system, the nos is mixed with fuel before entering the engine....  How does this happen?? (Ie - Where does the fuel come from??).||||reg||1
Z||000010||Alex4mula||12-11-2001||03:45 PM||||Tube from shrader valve at regulator body to TBS.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||12-12-2001||09:49 AM||||it's not nos.. it's N2O. or nitrous, or nitrous oxide. not nos thats a name brand.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Turbo88||12-12-2001||11:57 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GodSend:<BR><B>I see.  This may be a stupid question.  In a dry system, the nos comes from the bottle and is injected into the intake "as is" - In a wet system, the nos is mixed with fuel before entering the engine....  How does this happen?? (Ie - Where does the fuel come from??).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My understanding is that a wet system injects the extra fuel needed to burn with the N2O within a self contained system. A dry system uses the cars computer to add the required fuel to the mixture. With no dry kit experience, *I* thought that in a dry kit, you only plumb for and install the nitrous (lines, ect) where as a wet kit requires you to plumb for and install the fuel lines and associated hardware..<P>Oh yeah... NOS is a ricer term for Nitrous Oxide Systems. One of the more popular manufactures of nitrous systems and components. N2O is the chemical symbol for Nitrous Oxide which means there are two nitrogen atoms and one oxygen atom per molicule...<P><BR>Sorry, I had too <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">...<p>[This message has been edited by Turbo88 (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||GodSend||12-12-2001||12:17 PM||||ok then N20 !  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">....  What sort of method is used to tap into the fuel system?  Is a t-fitting, or are you supposed to drill and tap a hole somewhere?||||reg||1
Z||000014||FieroPerformance||12-12-2001||02:58 PM||||Alright. I've got a freshly rebuilt 2.8 (bored out so it is probably closer to 3.0). Everything is replaced but the crank, block, heads and connecting rods (they are all reconditioned though). I am running slightly higher compression power forged pistons, and the high performance cam from crane. The exhaust valves are opened up a little bit and the flange in the exhaust manifold is drilled out. No EGR.<P>What brand system should I look at, wet or dry, 75 shot or higher?, what should my quarter be? Any 0-60 time guesses?<P>I've got an Isuzu 5 speed.<P>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsigfperformance.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by FieroPerformance (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||1FST2M6||12-12-2001||03:31 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.nitrouswarehouse.com" TARGET=_blank>www.nitrouswarehouse.com</A>  <B>ONLY GET THIS KIT!</B> NOS, ZEX, Edelbrock are all ok but for the best system get the Nitrous Express System from nitrouswarehouse. it's $449 plus shipping. with a mildly built engine put a 52/22 jetting and hang on. thats what Reddog has i gave him aparently a mis drilled jet but i've already sent th ecorrect one out to him he's making an additional 90hp at the wheels and from what i understand loving every minute of it. or for a more mild shot a 41/20. 73hp... <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fst2m6sig_1.gif"> <P> <A HREF="http://www.1fst2m6.com" TARGET=_blank>www.1fst2m6.com</A>  - underconstruction - but check it out anywho...||||reg||1
Z||000016||reddog||12-12-2001||08:02 PM||||Yep. I love my NX "WET" system. It is definately worth the money . You probably should go ahead and look into getting a hi-performance ignition too. I bought a MSD 6A from Travis as well and he installed both in my GT a few weeks ago. The N2O really kicksa$$ & it pulls like crazy. Try it you'll like it!!!!!!!! BTW the wet kit does not require any special "Plumbing" you just attach a line to the schrader valve(Screws on) and screw the other end of the line into a shark valve which is attached to the "Bellows" just in front of the throttle body. If you give me a few minutes I'll go take a few pics of my system and post them. It will take a  little while because I am on a new computer that I just got for my home and have not downloaded the pip program yet. Be right back!!!!!<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
